@page "/runner"
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
<PageTitle>Run the actual C# client — Retry Assurance</PageTitle><span class="eyebrow">SOURCE, PACKAGE, REAL HTTP</span><h1>Your first completed job</h1><p class="lede">The examples invoke an actual C# create method once. Its internal retry makes real HTTPS requests to a capability-bound synthetic service.</p>
<section class="panel"><h2>1. Create and pin</h2><p><a href="/account">Create an actual account</a> and save its private recovery key. <a href="/projects/new">Create a project</a>, declare allowed terminal outcomes and pin <code>ra-core 1.0.0</code>. In that project's authorization section, create a capability for this runner.</p><p>A free account can own two projects and create fifty runs per UTC calendar month. No payment or production credential is required for this path.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>2. Build or download the working first-party clients</h2><p>Use the <a href="https://github.com/muhammetozeski/RetryAssurance/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener">portable release</a> with the .NET 10 runtime, or clone the <a href="https://github.com/muhammetozeski/RetryAssurance" target="_blank" rel="noopener">actual public repository</a> and build using the .NET 10 SDK. From the repository root:</p><pre>dotnet build examples/BrokenClient/BrokenClient.csproj -c Release
dotnet build examples/CorrectClient/CorrectClient.csproj -c Release</pre><p>The example client's JSON body is explicitly synthetic, and its SDK-style credentials are dummy values. No document is uploaded, fetched or converted by this scenario.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>3. Supply the project capability privately</h2><p>In PowerShell 7, read the capability without echoing it:</p><pre>$env:RA_PROJECT_TOKEN = Read-Host -MaskInput 'Project runner capability'</pre><p>For CI, use that platform's secret store. Never place a recovery key, project capability or production API key in command arguments, public logs or a Git commit. The runner stores no capability in its settings file.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>4. Observe the broken retry</h2><pre>@BrokenCommand</pre><p>The first real HttpClient request times out before acknowledgement. This intentionally broken client creates a new idempotency key inside its retry loop. One callback can therefore leave two durable remote jobs. The service derives <strong>fail</strong>, and the process exits with code 1.</p><p>This is a first-party executable example, not a claimed customer incident. An actual customer client without this fault may pass.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>5. Execute the corrected key ownership</h2><pre>@CorrectCommand</pre><p>The correct example creates the operation key outside its retry loop. Its second HTTP attempt reuses the first durable resource. The server checks that the successful typed client result refers to that real resource; an unrelated invented ID is incomplete.</p><p>The result's package/build labels are runner declarations. The service does not remotely attest arbitrary customer source or promise success when the declared contract permits indeterminate behavior.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>6. Compare a new run with the retained failure</h2><p>Read the run ID from the broken client's actual output. Start a separate corrected execution with the baseline and contract pin:</p><pre>@BaselineCommand</pre><p>Open the project, inspect the new run, and compare it with the baseline. The original record remains separate. Changing a behavior contract or pack requires explicit adoption; mismatched pins are labelled non-comparable.</p><p>Use <code>--json PRIVATE_OUTPUT_FILE</code> to save allowlisted evidence. Exit codes are 0 for server pass, 1 for server fail and 2 for incomplete or an action failure.</p></section>
<section class="split-panel"><div><h2>Adapt your actual SDK</h2><p>Inject <code>OperationContext.HttpClient</code> into the real SDK, provide dummy credentials and invoke its actual create method once. Map the returned typed resource ID or permitted terminal uncertainty to <code>ClientObservation</code>.</p><a href="https://github.com/muhammetozeski/RetryAssurance/blob/main/examples/ExampleClients/SyntheticJobClient.cs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the compiled first-party operation →</a></div><div><h2>Understand the transport boundary</h2><p>The supplied transport allows only the issued synthetic JSON POST/jobs destination, disables redirects and replaces general/content headers with an explicit allowlist. It does not sanitize arbitrary body content or sandbox code that creates its own HTTP transport.</p><a href="/privacy">Read the actual boundary →</a></div></section>
@code{
string Origin=>new Uri(Navigation.BaseUri).GetLeftPart(UriPartial.Authority);
string BrokenCommand=>$"dotnet examples/BrokenClient/bin/Release/net10.0/BrokenClient.dll --server {Origin} --commit YOUR_ACTUAL_COMMIT";
string CorrectCommand=>$"dotnet examples/CorrectClient/bin/Release/net10.0/CorrectClient.dll --server {Origin} --commit YOUR_ACTUAL_COMMIT";
string BaselineCommand=>$"dotnet examples/CorrectClient/bin/Release/net10.0/CorrectClient.dll --server {Origin} --commit YOUR_NEW_ACTUAL_COMMIT --baseline ACTUAL_BROKEN_RUN_ID --revision 1";
}
